# Local Terraform state and backend lock — should always be remote.
.terraform/
.terraform.lock.hcl
# ...with one carve-out: staging's DEPENDENCY lock is checked in, and it is not
# a "backend lock" at all — it pins provider versions + checksums. Without it CI
# re-resolves `~> 5.70` / `~> 3.6` / `~> 2.4` against the registry on every run,
# so a future provider release can turn qontinui-ci.yml red with no commit to
# this repo. That workflow is the only thing satisfying coord's candidate CI for
# qontinui-stack, so a spontaneous red wedges one of COORD_MERGE_SLOTS. It
# carries linux_amd64 AND windows_amd64 checksums (`terraform providers lock
# -platform=...`) so `-lockfile=readonly` holds on CI and on the operator box.
!staging/.terraform.lock.hcl
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan
crash.log

# Real tfvars carry secrets; only the .example is checked in.
*.tfvars
!*.tfvars.example
# ...with one deliberate carve-out. staging/production.auto.tfvars holds exactly
# one non-secret value — first_superuser_email, an address already committed in
# staging/terraform.tfvars.example — and exists so `terraform plan` runs with no
# tfvars and no -var, which is the property plan
# 2026-08-04-terraform-secretless-input-surface shipped and which a defaultless
# required variable would otherwise break. Keep it secret-free; anything that IS
# a secret belongs in Secrets Manager or SSM and reaches terraform as a data
# source, per that plan.
!staging/production.auto.tfvars
